I checked the official product specification and could not refute the finding. The document is the "EGRI BIKAVÉR oltalom alatt álló eredetmegjelölés termékleírása", original version, published on boraszat.kormany.hu. Egri Bikavér is now its own PDO, and this specification applies to wines marketed from the 2023/2024 wine year.

Section III sets the same blending rules for Classicus, Superior and Grand Superior:
- at least four varieties ("legalább négy szőlőfajta"), each above 5%;
- Kékfrankos between 30% and 65% ("30 és 65% között") and the largest component ("ennek a fajtának kell a legnagyobb arányban jelen lennie");
- Turán (and Bíbor kadarka for Classicus) capped at 10%.

There is no longer a 50% cap on any single variety, since Kékfrankos may reach 65%, and Superior does not require five varieties. Both clauses in the model answer come from earlier Eger PDO rules, where the minimum was three varieties and no variety could pass 50% (hungarianwines.eu still describes those old rules). The 30-65%, four-variety rule was already in the Eger specification when egriborut.hu summarised it in March 2021. It was carried into the stand-alone Egri Bikavér PDO specification from the 2023/2024 wine year. Kékfrankos is still the keyed grape and the accept list (kekfrankos, blaufrankisch) is fine. The only problem is the outdated detail in the model answer, and the examiner's fix states the current rule correctly.
